<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    ============================================================
    MI ARCUS — IIS REQUEST SIZE / TIMEOUT CONFIG
    ============================================================

    WHY THIS FILE EXISTS
    =====================
    client/src/axiosConfig.js documents the real production path for
    this app:

        https://rytual2.miarcus.com/api/*
                       |
                       v  (IIS reverse proxy)
        http://127.0.0.1:5000/api/*

    IIS's OWN default request-size limit (maxAllowedContentLength) is
    30,000,000 bytes — about 28.6 MB. That limit is enforced by IIS
    itself, BEFORE the request ever reaches the Node/Express app on
    127.0.0.1:5000. A bulk-upload file under ~28 MB sails through; a
    bigger one is rejected by IIS with "413 Request Entity Too Large"
    before Express's own body size guards, multer, or any application
    code ever runs.

    THE APP'S OWN UPLOAD CEILING IS NOW 100 GB — BUT READ THIS FIRST
    ====================================================================
    The Node application itself now allows uploads up to 100 GB (see
    MAX_UPLOAD_SIZE in server/middleware/fileSecurity.js). That number
    is NOT, by itself, enough to make a 100 GB upload work through
    this IIS reverse proxy, because of one hard technical fact:

        IIS's request-filtering "maxAllowedContentLength" setting is a
        32-bit unsigned integer. Its absolute maximum possible value is
        4,294,967,295 bytes — about 4 GB (4,294,967,296 minus 1). There
        is no higher number IIS will accept for this setting, and no
        "unlimited" value exists for it. This is true of every IIS
        installation everywhere; it is not something this app, or any
        config file, can change.

    That means: a single, ordinary HTTP upload larger than ~4 GB CANNOT
    pass through this IIS site no matter what is configured below. The
    setting is already raised to that literal ceiling (see below) —
    there is nothing higher to set it to.

    HOW FILES BETWEEN ~4 GB AND 100 GB ACTUALLY GET THROUGH
    ============================================================
    For anything larger than IIS's ~4 GB ceiling, this app uses a
    CHUNKED UPLOAD flow instead of one giant request:
      - server/middleware/chunkedUpload.js + server/routes/uploadRoutes.js
        (mounted at /api/uploads) accept the file as many smaller
        pieces (the client defaults to 500 MB pieces — see
        client/src/components/common/BulkUploadModal, the
        `enableChunkedUpload` / `chunkSize` props), each comfortably
        under IIS's per-request ceiling, and reassemble them on the
        server into the original file before handing it to the normal
        bulk-upload controller.
      - This is currently wired up for the two bulk-upload features
        that were failing in production: Checklist Reports
        (POST /api/checklist-reports/bulk-upload) and Action Points
        (POST /api/action-points/bulk-upload). Any file up to the app's
        100 GB ceiling on either of those two screens will now
        automatically use the chunked path once it is bigger than the
        chunk threshold, with NO single request ever exceeding IIS's
        limit.
      - Every OTHER upload in this app (attachments, gallery, general
        CSV imports on other pages) still goes through as one request,
        so those remain limited in practice to whatever fits under
        IIS's ~4 GB ceiling in one shot, even though the app-level
        number now allows up to 100 GB. Apply the same
        enableChunkedUpload pattern to another page's bulk-upload route
        if it also needs to accept files bigger than ~4 GB.

    UNLIMITED-AS-POSSIBLE REQUEST SIZE (single-request path)
    ============================================================
    The application itself no longer imposes an upload size ceiling
    below 100 GB:
      - server/middleware/bulkFileUpload.js / csvUpload.js -> multer
        fileSize limit raised to MAX_UPLOAD_SIZE (100 GB)
      - server/routes/checklistReportRoutes.js /
        server/routes/actionpointRoutes.js -> both now share the same
        4-hour request timeout (server/middleware/extendUploadTimeout.js)
        so a very large single-request transfer has time to fully
        upload + parse + import.
      - server/middleware/requestSecurity.js's contentTypeGuard already
        exempts multipart/form-data uploads from its own 10 MB check

    IIS itself has no literal "unlimited" setting for
    maxAllowedContentLength — it's a 32-bit value, so the highest it
    can be set to is 4,294,967,295 bytes (4 GB), which is what this
    file now uses. That is IIS's own hard ceiling; there is no higher
    value to configure. In practice this is as close to "unlimited" as
    a SINGLE IIS-fronted request can get — see the chunked upload
    section above for how this app gets past that ceiling for files
    that need to.

    IIS's own ceiling is the one piece that lives outside this Node
    project entirely (IIS reads it from web.config / applicationHost.config,
    not from anything Node/Express does), so it has to be raised here —
    and, critically, DEPLOYED to the actual IIS site (see below). Simply
    having this file in the repo does nothing on its own; the numbers
    below only take effect once they're applied to the IIS site that
    fronts rytual2.miarcus.com.

    THE OTHER LIKELY CAUSE OF "502 Bad Gateway" ON BULK UPLOADS
    ============================================================
    A 502 on a bulk upload that is NOT near any size ceiling (for
    example, a 27 MB CSV) is almost never a size problem — it's
    Application Request Routing (ARR) giving up on waiting for the
    Node app's response before the app has actually finished
    processing the file (parsing + validating + importing thousands of
    rows can legitimately take longer than ARR's default ~30 second
    proxy timeout). This file now sets an explicit, much longer
    <proxy timeout="..."> (see below) to fix that specific failure
    mode, kept in sync with the Node-side timeouts above.

    HOW TO APPLY THIS
    ===================
    1. This file needs to live in the IIS SITE ROOT that terminates
       https://rytual2.miarcus.com and reverse-proxies /api/* to
       127.0.0.1:5000 (via URL Rewrite / Application Request Routing).
       That site almost certainly already has its own web.config with
       the URL Rewrite rules in it — if so, do NOT just drop this file
       on top of it. Instead, copy the <system.webServer> and
       <system.web> content below into the EXISTING web.config (merge
       the <security>/<serverRuntime>/<httpProtocol>/<proxy> blocks and
       the <httpRuntime> block in; keep the existing <rewrite> rules as
       they are).
    2. If that IIS site has no web.config yet (e.g. it is a bare
       reverse-proxy site), this file can be used as-is.
    3. After saving, either recycle the site's Application Pool or run
       `iisreset` for the new limits to take effect.
    4. Application Request Routing (ARR) also needs "Enable proxy" and
       needs Application Request Routing installed as an IIS module for
       the <proxy> element below to have any effect. If ARR is used as
       a SERVER-WIDE proxy (rather than per-site URL Rewrite rules),
       ARR also has its own separate content-length limit under IIS
       Manager -> Application Request Routing Cache -> Server Proxy
       Settings -> "Maximum server response content buffer size" /
       (on older IIS, a "maxAllowedContentLength" set on the ARR proxy
       module itself). If uploads still fail after steps 1-3, raise
       that limit too.
    5. If a 413/502/timeout still happens above the app's own layer
       after all of the above, check for a CDN, load balancer, or WAF
       in front of IIS (e.g. Cloudflare) — those commonly have their
       own, separate upload size ceiling AND proxy timeout that also
       have to be raised. Cloudflare's free/pro plans, for example, hard
       -cap a single request body at 100 MB regardless of anything
       configured here — if this app sits behind Cloudflare (or a
       similar edge proxy), the chunked upload flow described above is
       not just a workaround for IIS, it's required.
-->
<configuration>
    <system.webServer>

        <!-- ==================================================
             REQUEST SIZE LIMIT
             Default is 30,000,000 bytes (~28.6 MB). Raised to
             4,294,967,295 bytes (~4 GB) — the maximum this IIS
             setting supports; there is no literal "unlimited"
             value available. Files larger than this MUST use the
             chunked upload flow described above.
        =================================================== -->
        <security>
            <requestFiltering>
                <requestLimits maxAllowedContentLength="4294967295" />
            </requestFiltering>
        </security>

        <!-- ==================================================
             UPLOAD READ-AHEAD SIZE
             Governs how much of the request body IIS buffers
             before handing it to the module doing the proxying.
             The IIS default here (49152 bytes / 48 KB) is far
             smaller than a real bulk-upload file — raised to the
             same maximum as the request size limit above so it
             never becomes a second, smaller ceiling.
        =================================================== -->
        <serverRuntime uploadReadAheadSize="4294967295" />

        <!-- ==================================================
             APPLICATION REQUEST ROUTING (ARR) PROXY TIMEOUT
             ARR's own default response wait time is roughly 30
             seconds — far shorter than a bulk import that has to
             parse and write tens/hundreds of thousands of rows,
             or a multi-hundred-MB chunk of a very large upload.
             This is very likely the actual cause of "502 Bad
             Gateway" on a bulk upload that is nowhere near any
             size ceiling (e.g. a 27 MB file): IIS gives up waiting
             on Node before Node has finished, and returns 502 to
             the browser even though the app itself is still
             working (or already succeeded) in the background.

             Raised to 4 hours to match:
               - server.js -> httpServer.requestTimeout
               - middleware/extendUploadTimeout.js -> UPLOAD_TIMEOUT_MS
             responseBufferLimit="0" disables ARR's response
             buffering so large JSON responses (e.g. a bulk-upload
             result listing thousands of skipped rows) are streamed
             straight through instead of being buffered in memory
             with its own separate size ceiling.

             Requires the Application Request Routing IIS module to
             be installed and "Enable proxy" turned on at the
             server level for this element to take effect — see
             HOW TO APPLY THIS, step 4, above.
        =================================================== -->
        <proxy enabled="true" timeout="04:00:00" responseBufferLimit="0" />

        <httpProtocol>
            <customHeaders>
                <remove name="X-Powered-By" />
            </customHeaders>
        </httpProtocol>

    </system.webServer>

    <!-- ==================================================
         CLASSIC ASP.NET REQUEST LIMITS
         Some IIS setups (or the site's existing web.config)
         still enforce maxRequestLength/executionTimeout from
         the classic <system.web> pipeline in addition to (or
         instead of) <system.webServer>'s requestFiltering
         above. maxRequestLength is capped at 2,097,151 KB
         (~2 GB) — the largest value this setting accepts — and
         executionTimeout is raised to 14400 seconds (4 hours)
         to match the Node route timeouts and the ARR proxy
         timeout above.
    =================================================== -->
    <system.web>
        <httpRuntime maxRequestLength="2097151" executionTimeout="14400" />
    </system.web>
</configuration>
